+\subsection{What independent observations identify: sharp static bounds}\label{sec:c13ident}
+
+The ambiguity in Proposition~\ref{prop:mask}(iii) can be quantified without introducing another dynamic experiment. Write $u=\Ups(q)$. Suppose total area $L=A_{tot}$, the minimum $a_{\min}=\Acmin$, and the current fast-land yield $b_f$ are known; assume independently justified, continuously attainable yield limits $0<u_-\le u\le u_+<b_f$. The only allocation restrictions used here are $A_f,A_r\ge0$, $A_c\ge a_{\min}$, and $A_f+A_c+A_r=L$. The yield interval must be attainable independently of the permitted areas; additional joint restrictions require a different feasible set.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Sharp static identification intervals]\label{prop:c13ident}
+For a feasible observed $B$, the exact set of compatible capital-land areas using $B$ alone is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:c13boundsB}
+\mathcal I_B=\left[a_{\min},\ \min\left\{L,\frac{B}{u_-},\frac{b_fT-B}{b_f-u_+}\right\}\right].
+\end{equation}
+If $A_f$ is also independently known, put $C=B-b_fA_f$. For $C\ge0$, the exact set is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:c13boundsAf}
+\mathcal I_{B,A_f}=\left[\max\left\{a_{\min},\frac{C}{u_+}\right\},\ \min\left\{L-A_f,\frac{C}{u_-}\right\}\right].
+\end{equation}
+An interval with lower endpoint above its upper endpoint is infeasible; $C<0$ is also infeasible. Independently knowing $q$ reduces the latter set to $A_c=C/\Ups(q)$ if this value satisfies the allocation restrictions. These are sharp static sets, not sets of dynamically reachable states.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+At fixed $A_c=a$, varying the admissible $u$ and $A_f$ gives exactly $u_-a\le B\le b_f(L-a)+u_+a$. Solving these inequalities gives \eqref{eq:c13boundsB}. When $A_f$ is fixed, $u_-a\le C\le u_+a$ gives \eqref{eq:c13boundsAf}. Continuity and attainment of the yield interval supply a feasible allocation at each permitted $a$; SI~\S S9 gives the construction and endpoint witnesses.
+\end{proof}
+
+For a model-scale illustration, take $b_f=0.85$\,gha\,ha$^{-1}$, $L=2.5$\,ha, $a_{\min}=0.05$\,ha, $u\in[0.05,0.066]$\,gha\,ha$^{-1}$, and $B=0.825$\,gha. The baseline yield law attains this entire interval on $q\in[0,1]$. If an independent observation gives $A_f=0.785/0.85=0.923529\ldots$\,ha, then $C=0.04$\,gha. Table~\ref{tab:c13bounds} shows how the additional observations narrow the compatible set. Even the middle interval crosses an arbitrarily chosen $0.7$\,ha reporting cut; that cut is not an ecological threshold. Independently observing $q=0.9$ identifies $A_c=0.717360\ldots$\,ha in this example. Observing only $\Ups$ need not identify $q$, because this yield law is not one-to-one. The illustration is not an estimate from NFA accounts. Uncertain yields or measurements require projection of a coherent joint uncertainty set, rather than treating these deterministic intervals as confidence intervals.
